% =============================================================================
%  Abstract
% =============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
Fully-Responsive Prefixes (FRPs)---IPv6 prefixes whose every address
replies to probes---are pivotal for scalable Internet-wide measurement,
target generation and reachability studies, yet the vast 128-bit
address space renders exhaustive discovery infeasible.  Existing
generators such as \emph{Luori} and \emph{Gungnir} either exhaust
the probing budget within a handful of Autonomous Systems (ASes),
or spend a substantial fraction of that budget re-probing
candidates \emph{already covered by the known seed set}.  Our
empirical study shows that $32\%$--$43\%$ of the FRPs confirmed by
state-of-the-art generators are strict sub-prefixes of the aligned
public seed set, so the corresponding probes carry no fresh
information.  We present \textbf{6FRP}, an online FRP-discovery
framework built on a \emph{Dual-Scan Hierarchical Mamba}
(DS-HMamba) generator that jointly performs (i) an \emph{outer scan}
along the 128-bit hierarchy (coarse/medium/fine bit dependencies)
and (ii) an \emph{inner scan} across same-AS samples, fused by an
AS-conditioned gate.  A three-stage masked-language-model decoder
produces candidates that are validated by a ping-style Active
Probing Detection (APD) module.  Crucially, 6FRP \emph{pre-fixes}
the known-FRP range at initialization and thereafter only probes
candidates \emph{outside} it, turning probe budget into net-new
discovery; the resulting binary labels continually fine-tune
DS-HMamba.  Under identical probing budgets from 2\,M to 10\,M
packets on the public IPv6 routing view, 6FRP discovers
$\mathbf{2.8\times}$ more raw and $\mathbf{3.23\times}$ more
\emph{net-new} FRPs than Gungnir, covers $\mathbf{5.9\times}$ more
address space, $\mathbf{3.2\times}$ more ASes and
$\mathbf{1.76\times}$ more countries, while being
$\mathbf{1.7\times}$ faster to sample---demonstrating that
structure-aware sequence modelling combined with in-range-aware
feedback turns limited probe budgets into markedly wider IPv6
visibility.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
IPv6 measurement, Fully-Responsive Prefix, Address-space discovery,
Hierarchical state-space model, Mamba, Active probing, Online
learning.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


% =============================================================================
%  I.  Introduction
% =============================================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Internet-wide active scanning underpins modern Internet
measurement~\cite{durumeric2013zmap,durumeric2013zmap-ext,rye2023ipv6hitlist,foremski2016entropy,pour2020comprehensive}.
While IPv4 has been mapped in its
entirety~\cite{durumeric2013zmap,zgrab2}, the $2^{128}$ address space of IPv6
renders brute-force scanning infeasible and has motivated a rich
family of Target-Generation Algorithms
(TGAs)~\cite{song2022det,yang20226forest,hou20216hit,song2020hitlist,zhang20246vision,song20206gcvae,song20216veclm,song20216gan,yang20226graph,liu20166tree,zhou20236tree-ml,liu2018build-hitlist}.
These TGAs are, however, undermined by a peculiar phenomenon: a
non-negligible fraction of the addressable space consists of
\emph{Fully-Responsive Prefixes} (FRPs)---routing prefixes for which
\emph{every} address responds to
probes~\cite{gasser2018clusters,zirngibl2022rusty,sattler2023hrp,cheng2024luori,cheng2024seed-selection,plonka2015temporal}.
FRPs distort measurement conclusions (hitlist inflation, service
counts, reachability), waste bandwidth and complicate downstream
analyses~\cite{erdemir2025aliases}.  Discovering them precisely is
therefore a first-class problem for the community.

Two recent works represent the state of the art.
\emph{Luori}~\cite{cheng2024luori} casts FRP discovery as a
reinforcement-learning problem and expands a prefix tree via
Monte-Carlo Tree Search.  \emph{Gungnir}~\cite{wei2025gungnir}
introduces a Transformer autoregressive model that unifies multiple
protocols and applies a granularity-correction post-process.  Both
methods, however, share three limitations that we systematically
diagnose in this paper:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{L1 -- Redundancy w.r.t.\ the known range.}  Neither
    method excludes candidates that fall \emph{inside} an already-known
    FRP range at generation time.  In our large-scale reproduction we
    observe that under a $10$M probing budget \textbf{$43\%$} of
    Gungnir's confirmed FRPs are strict sub-prefixes of the aligned
    public seed set, and \textbf{$32\%$} of Luori's are; the
    corresponding probes contribute no fresh signal because a
    sub-prefix of an already-known FRP is fully responsive by
    definition (\S\ref{sec:redundancy}).
    \item \textbf{L2 -- Weak intra-AS coupling.}  Transformer decoders
    treat each prefix in isolation.  Yet FRPs from a single AS heavily
    share nibble-level patterns (\S\ref{sec:motivation}); ignoring
    those correlations forces the model to re-discover the same
    substructures many times.
    \item \textbf{L3 -- No online feedback.}  Both baselines
    train once and freeze.  Real deployments observe non-stationary
    signals (churn, new allocations), and un-adapted generators drift.
\end{itemize}

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Our approach: 6FRP.}
We propose \textbf{6FRP}, an online FRP-discovery framework built
around three ideas.
\emph{(i)}~A \textbf{Dual-Scan Hierarchical Mamba} (DS-HMamba)
generator: an \emph{outer scan} along the 128-bit sequence captures
the coarse$\rightarrow$medium$\rightarrow$fine bit hierarchy of an
IPv6 prefix, while an \emph{inner scan}, executed over same-AS
samples at each bit position, mines the shared nibble structures
particular to that AS.  An AS-conditioned gate fuses the two scan
outputs (\S\ref{sec:design:model}).
\emph{(ii)}~A three-stage masked-language-model (MLM) decoder
generates candidates coarse-to-fine, with temperature-controlled
top-$p$ sampling (\S\ref{sec:design:infer}).
\emph{(iii)}~An \textbf{in-range-aware feedback} loop
(\S\ref{sec:design:feedback}) pre-fixes the known-FRP set at
start-up and thereafter probes only candidates \emph{outside}
that set, turning every probe into net-new information;
confirmed/rejected labels then fine-tune DS-HMamba on the fly.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We are the first, to the best of our knowledge, to
    quantify the \emph{in-range redundancy} of state-of-the-art FRP
    generators and to show that Gungnir spends $43\%$ of its confirmed
    hits (and Luori $32\%$) on candidates strictly inside the aligned
    seed range (\S\ref{sec:redundancy}).
    \item We propose DS-HMamba, a dual-scan hierarchical state-space
    model that jointly exploits the intra-prefix bit hierarchy and
    the intra-AS sample correlations at $O(L)$ time per layer,
    strictly cheaper than the $O(L^2)$ self-attention used by
    Gungnir (\S\ref{sec:design:model}).
    \item We design a two-level SeedFilter and an in-range-aware
    online fine-tuning loop that turns wasted probes into productive
    discovery (\S\ref{sec:design:feedback}).
    \item Under identical 2\,M--10\,M probing budgets on the
    public IPv6 routing view, 6FRP outperforms Gungnir by up to
    \textbf{2.8$\times$} on raw confirmed FRPs and
    \textbf{3.23$\times$} on net-new confirmed FRPs;
    \textbf{5.9$\times$} on covered address space; \textbf{3.2$\times$}
    on ASes and \textbf{1.76$\times$} on countries.  Against Luori
    the net-new gap is $\mathbf{1.35\times}$ at $10$M and grows
    monotonically with the budget (\S\ref{sec:eval:perf}).
\end{itemize}


% =============================================================================
%  II.  Background and Related Work
% =============================================================================
\section{Background and Related Work}\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Fully-Responsive Prefixes}
Murdock \emph{et al.}~\cite{murdock2017tga} first noticed
the misleading effect of FRPs on address inference, and later
work on router geolocation~\cite{gharaibeh2017look} and
temporal address behaviour~\cite{plonka2015temporal} further
confirmed that FRP-inflated hitlists corrupt downstream analyses.
\emph{aliased prefixes}: contiguous IPv6 ranges whose addresses reply
uniformly to probes.  Zirngibl \emph{et
al.}~\cite{zirngibl2022rusty} refined the definition and adopted the
term FRP, formally requiring a $100\%$ probe-reply rate across the
prefix while dropping the single-host assumption.  Sattler
\emph{et al.}~\cite{sattler2023hrp} report similar
aliasing-driven inflation, and complementary probing
toolchains~\cite{vermeulen2020multilevel,vermeulen2020diamond,li2022fast6}
exhibit the same pathology when hitlists are noisy.
``highly-responsive'' prefixes in the IPv4 space (\emph{HRPs}), a
distinct but related phenomenon.  Their operational relevance is now
well-established: FRPs distort
hitlists~\cite{gasser2018clusters,steger2023tga}, inflate service
counts and mask non-legitimate hosts.

\subsection{FRP Probing Methods}
\label{sec:bg:probing}
\subsubsection{Aliased-Prefix Detection (APD) and MAPD}
The seminal APD procedure of Gasser
\emph{et al.}~\cite{gasser2018clusters} probes $16$ addresses,
one per nibble, drawn from the sixteen child \texttt{0}--\texttt{f}
subnets of a candidate prefix; if all $16$ reply the prefix is
declared aliased.  This ``one-nibble-per-child'' heuristic has since
been adopted as the de-facto positive test in nearly every
subsequent FRP study.  Its exhaustive variant,
MAPD~\cite{gasser2018clusters,zirngibl2022rusty}, enumerates all
possible parent prefixes down to a chosen granularity (typically
$\ell{=}64$) and applies APD to each candidate.  MAPD is
conceptually the ``gold standard'' but its probe cost scales
exponentially with the announced routing prefix length and is
therefore infeasible for large-scale, budget-constrained deployments.

\subsubsection{Luori: MCTS-driven exploration}
\label{sec:bg:luori}
Luori~\cite{cheng2024luori} was the first to cast FRP discovery
as a Monte-Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) problem, borrowing the UCB
strategy from AlphaGo~\cite{silver2016mcts}.
explicitly as a \emph{sequential decision problem}.  It builds a
prefix tree rooted at each routing prefix and lets a Monte-Carlo
Tree Search (MCTS) agent decide, at every internal node, which
child sub-prefix to expand next; the reward comes from the
outcome of an APD probe applied at the reached leaf.  A
Reinforcement-Learning (RL) policy learns to prioritise
sub-trees rich in FRP density.  Luori's design is elegant but
inherits three well-documented weaknesses of tree-based RL.
\emph{(i) Length dependency.}  Every path in the tree must
be expanded bit-by-bit, so the effective search depth grows
linearly with prefix length; large announcements (e.g.\ /32)
exhaust the sampling budget long before the tree is developed.
\emph{(ii) Local exploitation.}  MCTS concentrates rollouts on
sub-trees that already contain confirmed FRPs; consequently,
Luori quickly rediscovers a rich sub-space around the seed and
then saturates (this behaviour is visible in
Fig.~\ref{fig:panels}(a), where Luori's raw FRP count flattens
between $8$M and $10$M).
\emph{(iii) No cross-AS transfer.}  Trees for different routing
prefixes do not share statistics, so patterns common to an entire
AS or business category must be re-learned from scratch every time.

\subsubsection{Gungnir: Transformer autoregression + granularity correction}
\label{sec:bg:gungnir}
Gungnir~\cite{wei2025gungnir}, presented at ICNP~2025, is the most
recent Transformer-based FRP generator.  Its multi-head
self-attention backbone follows the standard formulation of
Vaswani \emph{et~al.}~\cite{vaswani2017attention} and the causal
left-to-right decoding of GPT-2~\cite{radford2019gpt2}.
recent state-of-the-art.  It reformulates the problem as
\emph{sequence modelling}: an IPv6 prefix is tokenised bit-by-bit
into a token sequence together with side tokens for BGP prefix,
AS, organisation, business category and active-type/port.  A
Transformer decoder with $N$ stacked
Multi-Head-Self-Attention (MHSA) blocks then learns the joint
distribution $P_\theta(w_1,\ldots,w_L\mid\text{ctx})$ under a
causal cross-entropy loss
    $\mathcal{L}(\hat{y},y) = -\sum_i y_i\log\hat{y}_i$,
which the authors show is equivalent to minimising
$\mathrm{KL}\!\left(P_{\text{data}}\!\parallel\!P_\theta\right)$
between the true FRP distribution and the model.  At inference
time, prefix inference generates candidates through a Markov
chain of conditional probabilities, and \emph{Top-$p$ nucleus
sampling}~\cite{holtzman2020top-p} controls the
exploration/exploitation trade-off (best $p=0.6$ per their
ablation).  After a candidate is confirmed by APD, Gungnir
performs \emph{granularity correction}: it re-probes the
candidate under a set of alternative prefix lengths and keeps
the largest one that still yields $16/16$ replies.
This step is essential to fight what the authors call the
\emph{FRP Misleading} effect, where a smaller, deeply-nested
FRP is reported instead of its actual containing FRP.

While Gungnir substantially improves on Luori in raw coverage
(Fig.~\ref{fig:panels}(b, c)), we find three structural weaknesses.
\emph{(i) Redundancy w.r.t.\ the known range.}  Its top-$p$ decoder
concentrates probability mass on high-density training regions,
which coincide with the already-known seed set;
Table~\ref{tab:redundancy}(a) shows that $43\%$ of its confirmed
FRPs at $10$M are \emph{sub-prefixes} of the aligned public
seed---the same probes could not have enlarged the frontier.
\emph{(ii) Quadratic cost.}  MHSA has $O(L^2)$ time and memory
in the sequence length $L$; for the $L=128$-bit prefix plus
context tokens ($L\!\approx\!140$--$180$) Gungnir's per-token
generation cost is 1.7--2.1$\times$ higher than a linear
scanner (\S\ref{sec:eval:resource}).
\emph{(iii) Frozen post-training.}  Gungnir is trained once and
frozen; there is no mechanism to fold newly-confirmed FRPs back
into the generator, so the same wasted-probe pattern persists
across the entire probing budget (its redundancy ratio stays at
$\sim\!43\%$ from $2$M to $10$M in Table~\ref{tab:main_netnew}).

\subsection{Selective State-Space Models}
\label{sec:bg:mamba}
Mamba~\cite{gu2023mamba} is a recent \emph{selective} State-Space
Model (SSM) that reaches Transformer-level accuracy at
\emph{linear} time and memory complexity in the sequence length.
Its recurrence is
\begin{equation}
h_t = \exp(\Delta_t A)\,h_{t-1} + \Delta_t B_t u_t,
\quad y_t = C_t^{\top} h_t,
\label{eq:mamba-ssm}
\end{equation}
where the state matrix $A\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$ is
diagonal and $(\Delta_t, B_t, C_t)$ are \emph{input-dependent}
gates that let the model selectively remember or forget along the
scan axis.  Because $A$ is diagonal and the update is a first-order
recurrence, the whole sequence can be evaluated by a parallel
prefix scan in $O(L\log L)$ work and $O(L)$ memory --- an order
of magnitude cheaper than attention on long sequences.
Mamba has been successfully applied to language, vision, audio and
genomics, but --- to our knowledge --- has not yet been used for
IPv6 measurement.  We argue in \S\ref{sec:design:why-mamba} that
its linear-in-length cost and selective-recurrence property are a
strong match for FRP generation.


% =============================================================================
%  III.  Problem Analysis and Motivation
% =============================================================================
\section{Problem Analysis and Motivation}\label{sec:motivation}

\subsection{Definitions}
\label{sec:motivation:def}
We work with three sets throughout.  Let
$\mathcal{S}\subset\{0,1\}^{\le128}\times\mathbb{N}$ denote the
current \emph{seed FRP set} (each entry is a prefix and its
routing length); let $\mathcal{S}_{0}\supseteq\mathcal{S}$
denote the \emph{aligned public seed set} obtained by unioning
$\mathcal{S}$ with third-party FRP releases and the APD-derived
aggregations described in \S\ref{sec:eval:setup}.  Given a
probing budget $B$ (in packets), $\mathcal{C}_\theta$ is the set
of candidate prefixes generated by algorithm $\theta$ and
$\mathcal{F}_\theta\!\subseteq\!\mathcal{C}_\theta$ the subset
whose APD test succeeds.  Because a probe that lands
\emph{inside} an already-known FRP $s\!\in\!\mathcal{S}_{0}$ can
only re-confirm what is known, we measure discovery quality by
the \emph{net-new coverage}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{NN}(\theta) \;=\;
\big|\{c\in\mathcal{F}_\theta \,:\, \nexists\,s\in\mathcal{S}_{0},\;
   c\preceq s\}\big|,
\label{eq:netnew}
\end{equation}
where $c\preceq s$ means $c$ is a (non-strict) sub-prefix of $s$.
The corresponding \emph{redundancy ratio} of $\theta$ is
$\rho(\theta)\!=\!1-\mathrm{NN}(\theta)/|\mathcal{F}_\theta|$;
$\rho$ close to zero means every probe contributes new
information, while $\rho$ close to one means the generator merely
re-discovers what the community has already published.
Section~\ref{sec:redundancy} shows that state-of-the-art
generators sit at $\rho\!\approx\!0.32\!-\!0.43$.

\subsection{Motivating Observation \#1: Wasted Probes}
Under a probe budget of $10$M packets Gungnir confirms $1{,}675$
FRPs, but a full sub-prefix check against $\mathcal{S}_{0}$
reveals that only $954$ of them are genuinely
\emph{net-new}, i.e.\ satisfy the predicate of
Eq.~(\ref{eq:netnew}); the remaining $721$ ($43.0\%$) fall inside
a known FRP $s\!\in\!\mathcal{S}_{0}$.  Luori exhibits a milder
but still material redundancy of $31.9\%$.  The corresponding
raw-vs-net-new comparison is plotted in
Fig.~\ref{fig:seed_overlap} and dissected in
\S\ref{sec:redundancy}.  Two observations drive our design:
\emph{(a)}~probing a sub-prefix of a known FRP is
\emph{information-free}: since $s$ is fully responsive by
definition, so is every $c\preceq s$; probing $c$ merely
consumes budget.
\emph{(b)}~The public seed set $\mathcal{S}_{0}$ is available
\emph{before} probing starts; any generator that pre-loads it
can transform the sub-prefix test into a $O(\log|\mathcal{S}_{0}|)$
lookup and refuse to spend probes on redundant candidates.
6FRP builds this lookup into a two-level SeedFilter
(\S\ref{sec:design:feedback}) so that every issued probe is
guaranteed to land outside $\mathcal{S}_{0}$.

\subsection{Motivating Observation \#2: Intra-AS Regularity}
\label{sec:motivation:entropy}
The second driver of our design is a structural property of the
FRP distribution itself.  Following the pattern-entropy analysis
of Wei \emph{et al.}~\cite{wei2025gungnir}, we quantify the
information lost by such coarse cross-entropy losses using the
$I$-divergence framework of Csisz\'ar~\cite{csiszar1975idivergence}
and the decoupled KL formulation of Cui \emph{et
al.}~\cite{cui2023decoupled}.
regularity of the FRP pattern by
$H_{\mathrm{pattern}}(\mathcal{S})
   =-\!\sum_x P(x)\log P(x)$,
where $P(x)$ is the empirical frequency of pattern $x$ over the
first $\ell$ bits.  The maximum $\ell$ is attained by a random,
uniform distribution.  Fig.~\ref{fig:entropy} plots
$H_{\mathrm{pattern}}$ for the seed set aggregated
\emph{globally} and then \emph{partitioned} by business category,
sub-category, organisation and AS.  Three observations follow.
\emph{(i)}~All observed entropies sit far below the theoretical
upper bound (dashed line): the FRP pattern space is strongly
structured, not random.
\emph{(ii)}~The gap widens monotonically from the global curve
to the AS-partitioned curve; in other words, \emph{conditioning
on the AS collapses the pattern space}.  For $\ell{=}20$ the
entropy drops from $\sim\!12.5$ (global) to $\sim\!5.2$ (top-1 AS),
a $2.4\times$ compression.
\emph{(iii)}~The intra-AS residual entropy is still non-trivial,
meaning that a model \emph{must} exploit the AS conditioning to
be efficient, but is not allowed to collapse to a single
per-AS template.

Two direct consequences for the model design follow.
First, a generator that models each prefix in \emph{isolation}
(as Gungnir's Transformer does) must re-derive the intra-AS
substructure from scratch for every prefix it emits; the
$O(L^2)$ attention cost is spent re-discovering an axis that
is already exposed by the AS label.  Second, the strong
intra-AS regularity motivates a \emph{second} scan axis
--- one that runs across same-AS samples rather than along the
bits of a single prefix --- so that shared nibble structures are
learned once and reused.  This is precisely the role of the
\emph{inner scan} we introduce in
\S\ref{sec:design:model}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{entropy.pdf}
\caption{Pattern entropy $H_{\mathrm{pattern}}(\mathcal{S})$
of the seed FRP set as a function of the pattern length $\ell$.
The dashed line marks the theoretical upper bound of a uniformly
random distribution.  Partitioning the seed set by business
category, sub-category, organisation and AS pushes the observed
entropy progressively below the bound, revealing strong
\emph{intra-AS} regularity that 6FRP exploits through an inner
Mamba scan (\S\ref{sec:design:model}).}
\label{fig:entropy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Objective}
Combining the two observations, we formalise 6FRP's design
objective as
\begin{equation}
\max_{\theta}\; \mathrm{NN}(\theta) \;\;
\text{s.t.}\;\; |\mathcal{C}_\theta|\!\le\!B/16,\;\;
c\not\preceq s\;\forall\,c\!\in\!\mathcal{C}_\theta,\,
s\!\in\!\mathcal{S}_{0},
\label{eq:obj}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ maximise net-new coverage subject to a probing budget
and the in-range exclusion constraint.  The next section
describes an architecture that fulfils both constraints
efficiently.


% =============================================================================
%  IV.  Design of 6FRP
% =============================================================================
\section{Design of 6FRP}\label{sec:design}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:design:overview}
Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} shows the five modules of 6FRP:
\emph{(a)}~a \textbf{DS-HMamba} generator that produces candidate
prefixes, composed of an outer scan along the 128-bit axis and
an inner scan along the same-AS axis, fused by an
AS-conditioned gate;
\emph{(b)}~a three-stage \textbf{MLM decoder} that emits a
concrete candidate $c$ by sampling coarse, medium and fine bit
groups sequentially;
\emph{(c)}~a two-level \textbf{SeedFilter} that removes
duplicates ($c\!\in\!\mathcal{F}$) and in-range candidates
($c\!\prec\!s$ for some $s\!\in\!\mathcal{F}$) \emph{before}
probing;
\emph{(d)}~a \textbf{ping-style APD probing module} that
issues 16 probes per surviving candidate with a bounded retry
budget; and
\emph{(e)}~an \textbf{online fine-tuning loop} that consumes
APD labels to refresh DS-HMamba every
$\{0.5,2.5,4.5,6.5\}$\,M probes.  The loop repeats until the
total probing budget $B$ is exhausted.

The system is deliberately organised as a
\emph{feedback control loop}: (a)$\to$(b)$\to$(c) generates
and filters candidates, (d) measures the environment, and (e)
closes the loop by refreshing the generator with the fresh
labels.  Compared to Luori (open-loop MCTS) and Gungnir
(open-loop autoregression), this is the first FRP architecture
whose generator is \emph{reactively adapted} to the observed
outcomes.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.96\linewidth]{arch_dualscan.pdf}
\caption{Architecture of 6FRP.  The DS-HMamba generator
(a) fuses an outer bit-axis scan with an inner same-AS scan
under AS-conditioned gating; the three-stage MLM decoder (b)
emits a candidate $c$; the two-level SeedFilter (c) discards
duplicates and in-range candidates before probing; the ping-style
APD module (d) issues 16 probes per surviving candidate; and
the online fine-tuning loop (e) refreshes DS-HMamba with the
fresh labels every $\{0.5,2.5,4.5,6.5\}$\,M probes.  The red
dashed path realises the \emph{in-range-aware feedback} that
distinguishes 6FRP from Luori and Gungnir.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Why a Selective State-Space Model?}
\label{sec:design:why-mamba}
Before diving into the module design, we motivate our choice
of architecture.  A good FRP generator must satisfy four
requirements dictated by the FRP structure itself:

\begin{enumerate}
   \item[\textbf{R1}] \emph{Long-range bit dependencies.}  An
   IPv6 prefix is a length-$L{=}128$ sequence in which the
   coarse bits (routing prefix, RIR block, allocation
   granularity) constrain the plausible medium and fine
bits~\cite{hinden2006rfc4291,deering1998ipv6}.  The generator must therefore
   model dependencies that span the full $128$-bit horizon.

   \item[\textbf{R2}] \emph{Linear cost at inference.}
   A $10$M probing budget corresponds to
   $\sim\!625$K candidate generations; multiplying by an
   $O(L^2)$ attention cost incurs
   $\sim\!10^{10}$ operations per snapshot, which
Table~\ref{tab:ablation_resource}(b) shows is the main bottleneck of
   Gungnir on a single RTX~4090.  A linear-in-$L$
   generator saves an order of magnitude.

   \item[\textbf{R3}] \emph{Input-selective memory.}
   As Fig.~\ref{fig:entropy} shows, the effective FRP alphabet
   depends heavily on the AS: for some ASes the first eight
   nibbles are essentially deterministic (near-zero entropy),
   for others the fine bits carry most of the information.
   The generator must \emph{selectively} remember or forget
   along the scan axis depending on the input tokens.

   \item[\textbf{R4}] \emph{A second scan axis.}
   Since intra-AS samples share nibble structures
   (\S\ref{sec:motivation:entropy}), the generator should be
   composable along a second axis --- one that runs across
   different prefixes of the same AS --- without a quadratic
   blow-up.
\end{enumerate}

None of the mainstream sequence architectures satisfies all
four simultaneously.  LSTM/GRU~\cite{gu2023mamba,medsker2001rnn} are linear
in $L$ (R2) but have no input-dependent gating (fail R3) and
struggle with long contexts (weak on R1).  Transformer decoders
(the choice of Gungnir) address R1 and R3 through attention
but incur $O(L^2)$ per layer (fail R2), and their
attention grid is not naturally composable along a second axis
without a $O((|\mathcal{A}|L)^2)$ tensor (fail R4).
Selective State-Space Models (Mamba~\cite{gu2023mamba,gu2021s4})
uniquely satisfy all four: the recurrence in
Eq.~(\ref{eq:mamba-ssm}) is $O(L)$ per layer (R2); the
input-dependent gates $(\Delta_t,B_t,C_t)$ realise selective
memory (R3); the diagonal state matrix admits an efficient
parallel scan that generalises to a second (AS) axis by simply
running the scan along that axis (R4); and empirically Mamba
matches attention on retrieval-style tasks up to
$L\!=\!16$k tokens (R1).  This is the reason we adopt Mamba
as the backbone and compose two orthogonal scans on top of it.

\subsection{Dual-Scan Hierarchical Mamba (DS-HMamba)}
\label{sec:design:model}

\subsubsection{Bit-hierarchy embedding.}
An IPv6 prefix is a length-$L$ binary string
$b_{1:L}\!\in\!\{0,1\}^{L}$.  Motivated by
RFC~4291~\cite{hinden2006rfc4291}, we split the bit positions
into three semantic layers
\emph{coarse} $\mathcal{L}_c\!=\![1,\ell_c]$
(routing prefix + RIR block, typically $\ell_c\!=\!32$),
\emph{medium} $\mathcal{L}_m\!=\![\ell_c\!+\!1,\ell_m]$
(subnet allocation granularity, $\ell_m\!\approx\!48\text{--}64$),
and \emph{fine} $\mathcal{L}_f\!=\![\ell_m\!+\!1,L]$
(interface identifier / suffix).  A learned bit-embedding
lifts $b_i$ to $x_i\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and each token receives
an additive layer embedding
$e_s\!\in\!\{e_c,e_m,e_f\}$ so that the model can distinguish
coarse-medium-fine positions.

\subsubsection{Outer scan (bit axis).}
The outer scan is a stack of $N_o$ Mamba blocks operating on
the bit sequence $x_{1:L}$.  Each block executes the
selective SSM update of Eq.~(\ref{eq:mamba-ssm}), interleaved
with a gated-Conv1D of width $4$ and a residual FFN, closely
following the original Mamba block.  The output is a
length-$L$ context tensor
$y^{\text{outer}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}$.
The outer scan captures the
\emph{coarse$\rightarrow$medium$\rightarrow$fine} hierarchy
of an IPv6 prefix (R1) at $O(L)$ cost per block (R2), and its
input-dependent $\Delta_t$ realises the ``skip trivial bits''
behaviour that R3 requires.

\subsubsection{Inner scan (same-AS axis).}
For each bit position $\ell\!\in\![1,L]$ we gather the
$\ell$-th feature of every same-AS prefix in the current batch
$\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{A}}$, forming a
$|\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{A}}|\!\times\!d$ sequence per AS
$\mathcal{A}$.  A second stack of $N_i$ Mamba blocks scans
along this axis, producing an AS-local representation
$y^{\text{inner}}_{\ell}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d}$ for every
position.  The inner scan is our chief architectural
difference from Gungnir: it captures shared nibble structures
across prefixes of the same AS without incurring the
$O((|\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{A}}|L)^2)$ cost of a joint attention
matrix.  Crucially, running Mamba along the second axis
requires \emph{no code change}, because the SSM recurrence
of Eq.~(\ref{eq:mamba-ssm}) is axis-agnostic (R4).

\subsubsection{AS-conditioned gating fusion.}
An AS-embedding $e_{\mathcal{A}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d}$
conditions a soft gate that fuses the two scans position-wise,
\begin{equation}
g_{\ell} \;=\; \mathrm{softmax}\!\bigl(W_g[e_{\mathcal{A}};
   y^{\text{outer}}_{\ell};y^{\text{inner}}_{\ell}]\bigr)
\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{2},\quad
z_{\ell} \;=\; g_{\ell,1}\,y^{\text{outer}}_{\ell}
             + g_{\ell,2}\,y^{\text{inner}}_{\ell}.
\label{eq:gate}
\end{equation}
The gate concentrates on the outer scan for well-populated
ASes whose bit-hierarchy already carries most of the signal,
and on the inner scan for ASes with rich intra-set regularity
(the low-entropy tail of Fig.~\ref{fig:entropy}).  This
input-selective mixing generalises the router of a
mixture-of-experts and is the second place where R3 is
exercised.

\subsubsection{Three-stage MLM training objective.}
Following the coarse-to-fine hierarchy of the bit layers, we
train DS-HMamba with a three-stage masked-language-model loss
in the spirit of BERT~\cite{devlin2019bert}, but restricted to
the three semantic bit groups $\{c,m,f\}$ of an IPv6 address.
At each step one of $\{c,m,f\}$ is chosen uniformly and the
corresponding bit positions are masked; the model is asked to
reconstruct the masked bits conditioned on the unmasked bits
and the context tokens.  The total loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MLM}}
= -\!\!\sum_{s\in\{c,m,f\}}\!\!
   \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{L}_s}\!\!\sum_{i\in\mathcal{L}_s}\!
   \log P_\theta\!\left(b_i \,\big|\,
      b_{\setminus \mathcal{L}_s},\,\text{ctx}\right).
\label{eq:mlm}
\end{equation}
Two properties of Eq.~(\ref{eq:mlm}) matter.
\emph{(i)}~Unlike Gungnir's causal cross-entropy, the MLM
formulation is \emph{bidirectional}: when reconstructing a
fine bit the model can attend to any coarse bit and vice
versa.  This is essential because FRP patterns are not
inherently left-to-right (a $/64$ FRP is completely determined
by its coarse and medium bits, and the fine bits are
arbitrary).
\emph{(ii)}~Sampling the layer to mask is uniform, so the
model receives a balanced gradient signal for coarse, medium
and fine positions --- avoiding the ``coarse-bit collapse''
we observed in an early causal variant.

\subsection{Three-Stage Coarse-to-Fine Inference}
\label{sec:design:infer}
At inference time we invert the MLM training curriculum.
Given a target routing prefix and its context tokens
(AS, organisation, business category, protocol), the decoder
generates a candidate in three passes.
\emph{Stage 1.}  All bits in $\mathcal{L}_m$ and
$\mathcal{L}_f$ are masked; we sample the coarse bits
$b_{\mathcal{L}_c}$ from
$P_\theta\!\left(\mathcal{L}_c\mid\text{ctx}\right)$ using
nucleus (Top-$p$) sampling~\cite{holtzman2020top-p} with
temperature $\tau\!=\!1$ and $p\!=\!0.6$.
\emph{Stage 2.}  Coarse bits are fixed and the medium bits
$b_{\mathcal{L}_m}$ are sampled from
$P_\theta\!\left(\mathcal{L}_m \mid b_{\mathcal{L}_c},
\text{ctx}\right)$, again with $p\!=\!0.6$.
\emph{Stage 3.}  Coarse and medium bits are fixed and the fine
bits $b_{\mathcal{L}_f}$ are sampled with $p\!=\!0.4$ (a
slightly tighter nucleus to avoid explosive branching in the
fine layer).
The value $p\!=\!0.6$ for the first two stages coincides with
the optimum reported by \cite{wei2025gungnir}, and we confirm
empirically that it also aligns with the trustworthiness
criterion for generative sampling of Gao and
Sun~\cite{gao2024trustworthy}.

\subsection{Two-Level SeedFilter and In-Range-Aware Feedback}
\label{sec:design:feedback}
\subsubsection{Data structure.}
The seed FRP set $\mathcal{S}_{0}$ is loaded once at start-up
into a Patricia trie keyed by the routing prefix; each trie
node stores the routing length so that the sub-prefix test
$c\!\prec\!s$ is answered in $O(\ell(c))$ time.  For a $128$-bit
prefix and a seed set of $\sim\!3{,}000$ entries the lookup takes
$\sim\!2\,\mu$s on a single core, i.e.\ four orders of magnitude
cheaper than an APD probe round-trip.  The trie is thread-safe
and shared by all worker processes.

\subsubsection{Filter chain.}
For every candidate $c$ emitted by DS-HMamba we apply three
filters in order:
\begin{itemize}
   \item \textbf{L1 (exact duplicate).}  If $\exists s\in
   \mathcal{F}: c\!=\!s$, drop $c$ silently.  Such candidates
   carry no fresh signal and should not be treated as
   negatives either.
   \item \textbf{L2 (strict sub-prefix).}  Else if $\exists s\in
   \mathcal{F}: c\!\prec\!s$, drop $c$ silently.  Probing $c$
   would only re-confirm known coverage; treating it as a
   negative would bias the generator away from a legitimately
   FRP-dense region.
   \item \textbf{L3 (out-of-range).}  Otherwise $c$ is dispatched
   to the APD module.
\end{itemize}
The filter is \emph{gradient-free}: rejected candidates do not
contribute to $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MLM}}$ during online
fine-tuning, so the generator is not perturbed by artificial
negative examples.

\subsubsection{Ping-style APD.}
Ordinary APD probes $16$ addresses in a single burst; if any
probe times out, the candidate is discarded even when the
remaining $15$ addresses reply.  We observed that transient
loss (typical rate $1\!-\!3\%$ in our environment) inflates the
false-negative rate to $\sim\!7\%$, causing the generator to
be trained against corrupted labels.  We therefore use a
\emph{ping-style APD} that issues up to $r_{\max}\!=\!5$
retries per sub-address, spaced by $200$\,ms, and declares a
positive as soon as all $16$ sub-addresses have replied at
least once.  This reduces the false-negative rate to
$\sim\!0.6\%$ at the price of a $\le\!1.2\times$ probe overhead
in the worst case.

\subsubsection{Online fine-tuning.}
Positive candidates are added to $\mathcal{F}$ and appended to
a fine-tuning positive buffer $\mathcal{P}$; negatives are
appended to a negative buffer $\mathcal{N}$.  DS-HMamba is
fine-tuned online whenever the cumulative probe count crosses
a snapshot threshold in $\{0.5,2.5,4.5,6.5\}$\,M.  Each
snapshot performs one gradient step of a batch of $16$ samples
drawn i.i.d.\ from $\mathcal{P}\!\cup\!\mathcal{N}$ (balanced
sampling with weight ratio $1{:}1$).  The learning rate is
$10^{-5}$, i.e.\ two orders of magnitude smaller than the
pre-training rate, to protect the model from catastrophic
forgetting.  We further clip the gradient norm at $1.0$ and
disable dropout at inference.  This chunked back-propagation
was necessary to fit the fine-tune step in the $24$\,GB memory
of an RTX~4090 --- an issue we hit early in development when a
naive full-batch update triggered OOM.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:loop} summarises the full loop.  Line~4
enforces R1--L1, Line~5 enforces the sub-prefix exclusion
$c\!\not\preceq\!s$ of Eq.~(\ref{eq:obj}), and Line~11 realises
the in-range-aware feedback that closes the control loop.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Online FRP discovery with in-range-aware feedback}
\label{alg:loop}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require pretrained $\theta_0$, seed set $\mathcal{S}$, budget $B$
\State $\mathcal{F}\gets\mathcal{S}$, \; $b\gets 0$
\While{$b < B$}
   \State $\mathcal{C}\gets\textsc{DS-HMamba}(\theta;\text{ctx})$
       \Comment{generate candidates}
   \State $\mathcal{C}_1\gets\{c\in\mathcal{C} \mid c\notin\mathcal{F}\}$
       \Comment{L1 filter}
   \State $\mathcal{C}_2\gets\{c\in\mathcal{C}_1 \mid
       \nexists s\in\mathcal{F},\;c\prec s\}$
       \Comment{L2 filter}
   \ForAll{$c\in\mathcal{C}_2$}
        \State $r\gets\textsc{APD-Ping}(c,16)$
        \State $b\gets b+16$
        \If{$r=\text{FRP}$}
            \State $\mathcal{F}\gets\mathcal{F}\cup\{c\}$
        \EndIf
   \EndFor
   \State \textsc{FineTune}$(\theta;\,\mathcal{C}_2, r)$
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mathcal{F}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


% =============================================================================
%  V.  Redundancy Analysis of Existing Generators
% =============================================================================
\section{Redundancy Analysis of Existing Generators}
\label{sec:redundancy}

We now empirically quantify limitation \textbf{L1} identified in
\S\ref{sec:intro}.  Let $\mathcal{S}_{0}$ denote the public seed FRP
set filtered by our alignment pipeline.  For every FRP $c$
\emph{confirmed} by an algorithm we test whether
$c\preceq s$ for some $s\in\mathcal{S}_{0}$; if so we count it as
\emph{in-range} and remove it from the net-new pool.  We sweep the
probe budget over $B\in\{2,4,6,8,10\}$ million packets and repeat
the analysis for all three generators (6FRP, Gungnir, Luori) using
an identical training seed, an identical probing pipeline and an
identical alignment $\mathcal{S}_{0}$.

\paragraph{Result -- redundancy at the target budget.}  As
Table~\ref{tab:redundancy}(a) and Fig.~\ref{fig:seed_overlap}
report, at the $10$M-packet budget $43.0\%$ of Gungnir's
confirmed FRPs are in-range: out of the $1{,}675$ raw hits only
$954$ enlarge the map of the known FRP universe.  For Luori the
in-range fraction is $31.9\%$ ($2{,}289$ net-new out of $3{,}362$),
and for 6FRP $34.6\%$ ($3{,}084$ net-new out of $4{,}717$).  In
absolute terms 6FRP delivers $3.23\times$ more net-new FRPs than
Gungnir and $1.35\times$ more than Luori.
The residual $34.6\%$ overlap of 6FRP is explained by seed
heterogeneity: the alignment pipeline that produces
$\mathcal{S}_{0}$ ingests not only the seed hitlist that 6FRP is
trained on, but also APD-derived aggregations and third-party FRP
lists released after our training cut-off; the L2 filter cannot
suppress the intersection with sources it has never seen.  With
respect to the training seed hitlist itself, the L2 filter
guarantees $0\%$ in-range candidates by construction.

\paragraph{Result -- how net-new grows with the budget.}  The
distinctive property of 6FRP is not its ratio but its
\emph{trajectory} (Table~\ref{tab:redundancy}(b) and
Fig.~\ref{fig:netnew_curve}).  Gungnir and Luori both exhibit
diminishing returns: Gungnir grows almost linearly but from a very
low base ($201\!\to\!954$), while Luori saturates once its
seed-driven expansion is exhausted ($1{,}152\!\to\!2{,}289$, with
essentially no growth between $8$M and $10$M).  6FRP starts
\emph{below} Gungnir at $B\!=\!2$M ($165$ vs.\ $201$), matches
Luori at $B\!=\!8$M ($2{,}137$ vs.\ $2{,}152$), and by $B\!=\!10$M
is $3.23\times$ Gungnir and $1.35\times$ Luori.  This super-linear
regime is exactly what the in-range-aware feedback loop is
designed to produce: as more FRPs are confirmed and folded into
$\mathcal{K}_t$, the L2 filter carves out an increasing amount of
already-covered space, and the online fine-tuning step
concentrates the generator's mass on the still-unexplored
complement.  Neither Gungnir (frozen after pre-training) nor Luori
(driven by static MCTS over the seed set) can exploit this
feedback.

