% =============================================================================
%  VII.  Discussion
% =============================================================================
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why in-range-aware feedback wins.}
Every probe issued by 6FRP is guaranteed to fall outside
$\mathcal{S}_{0}$.  A confirmed FRP therefore \emph{directly}
enlarges $\mathcal{S}_{0}$; a negative refines the generator away
from that region.  In contrast, Gungnir's Transformer
top-$p$ decoder tends to concentrate probability mass on
high-density training regions, which coincide with the seed
set: the same behaviour that helps it hit FRPs also makes it
re-hit \emph{known} FRPs.  This is why its redundancy ratio
stays essentially flat at $\sim\!43\%$ regardless of the budget,
while 6FRP's ratio drops from $93\%$ at $2$M to $35\%$ at $10$M
as the L2 filter progressively carves the seed range out of
the search space.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
6FRP has two limitations.
\emph{First}, the seed trie captures only the seed FRPs
available at start-up; FRPs released \emph{after} the training
cut-off are still probed as fresh candidates, producing the
residual $34.6\%$ overlap observed at $B{=}10$M
(\S\ref{sec:redundancy}).  A periodic seed refresh, coupled to
an external mirror of the community FRP database, would close
this gap.
\emph{Second}, DS-HMamba is pre-trained on the ICMPv6 protocol
only; extending to TCP/UDP requires re-training the outer/inner
scans (context tokens are already protocol-agnostic).

\paragraph{Generality.}
Although our experiments focus on ICMPv6, the DS-HMamba
architecture is protocol-agnostic and the feedback loop only
requires binary APD labels; TCP/UDP variants are a
straightforward extension.

\paragraph{Ethics.}
We follow the Menlo Report~\cite{menloreport} and community
best practices~\cite{partridge2016ethics,eddy2007synflood,guo2018icmp,izhikevich2022lzr,github-crypto-2021,google-v6-stats,gregor2015draw}: rate-limited
scanning ($\leq 1\,\text{Mbps}$), single measurement host,
an opt-out mailing list, and no penetration testing on any
identified host.


% =============================================================================
%  VIII.  Conclusion
% =============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
We revisit IPv6 FRP discovery and show that state-of-the-art
generators waste $32\%$--$43\%$ of their probing budget on
candidates already contained in the aligned public seed range.
Our solution, \textbf{6FRP}, combines a dual-scan hierarchical
selective-state-space generator with an in-range-aware online
feedback loop; under identical budgets it discovers
$2.8\times$ more raw FRPs and $3.23\times$ more \emph{net-new}
FRPs than the state-of-the-art Gungnir, covers $5.9\times$
more address space, $3.2\times$ more ASes and $1.76\times$
more countries, while remaining $1.7\times$ faster to sample.
Code, data and the enlarged FRP list will be released at
\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/6FRP} to foster further
IPv6 measurement research.  Future work will explore periodic
seed refresh against a live community FRP mirror and
multi-protocol extension to TCP/UDP active types.


